e asked for an interview with board  president dr john strobel  they didn't make him available so when  the board took a break  we tried to get some answers i'm jim  axelrod with cbs news  okay i'd like to ask you a couple of  questions of course i can't why can't  you uh we have a  hey mike yeah well you're the chairman  of the board and i would like to ask you  some questions about dr svabeck's case  mike can you direct him to the right  person you're the right person sir  you're in charge  are you telling me the buck doesn't stop  with you  now the mission of the indiana medical  licensing board is to protect the public  is the sanction just given to dr svavek  consistent with that  dr strobel didn't respond to a single  question we asked  he had nowhere to go including the  escalator jim i'm going to get nervous  next time i see you approaching with the  tv  crew but after all this reporting what  have you heard about why the medical  board seems so reluctant to pull the  doctor's licenses  when they know they're not doing a good  job  right right so that's the question gail  that we kept asking  throughout our reporting why with a  system in which two percent of the  doctors account for 50  percent of the malpractice awards and  settlements  why isn't there more vigilance on the  part of everyone in the system insurance  companies medical boards doctors  to target the bad actors and over and  over the experts  told us the same thing when you have  doctors policing doctors there's a  certain amount of reluctance built into  that system  to suspend or revoke licenses and the  degree  to which there are positions reserved  for non-doctors  on state medical boards is the degree to  which you will have  some independence and autonomy on that  board  right jim i have to say we were all  talking about your piece here at the  table we were really looking forward to  it and it certainly delivered  tony you touched on it yesterday as a  patient do we just have to rely on the  doctors to tell us the truth  is that how it goes well it's like it's  like what the what the man said in the  piece he said there should be a car  a carfax a carfax for for doctors if  hospitals can track it then people  should be able to track it and if  somebody in congress is watching this is  definitely something to take up in  legislation that information should be  public  yeah absolutely jim i forgot to say  thank you to you so thank you  thank you jim axelrod but thank you what  do patients do  if i'm so if we're supposed to have a  surgery just rely on the doctors to tell  us the truth  i think you're getting at something very  important here that national  practitioners data bank  let's open that up so that you can check  it out yes  thank you very much jim alex

